ID	Platform	Why It Matters	Test Cases Covered
AWS-001	AWS	Account boundaries define blast radius and the cross-account trust attack surface.	"TC1. From the tester federated identity in the management or audit account, the tester runs `aws organizations describe-organization`, `aws organizations list-roots`, `aws organizations list-organizational-units-for-parent`, `aws organizations list-accounts` and `aws organizations list-accounts-for-parent` to build a complete OU-and-account tree. Suspended accounts and accounts outside the main hierarchy are flagged. CloudFox `aws org` and Steampipe `aws_organizations_account` table are used to cross-check. Evidence: org-tree JSON, account inventory CSV with status / OU / owner columns.
TC2. From a tester role inside a member account (not the management account), the tester attempts `aws organizations list-accounts` and `aws organizations describe-organization` to confirm whether sibling-account enumeration is blocked by the org's policy. A correctly configured member account is denied. Evidence: CLI denial output, CloudTrail event ID for the AccessDenied event, finding spreadsheet."
AWS-002	AWS	Compromise of the management account compromises every member account.	"TC1. The tester verifies root-credential custody by reviewing the bank's sealed-envelope register and the IAM credential report for the management account: `aws iam get-credential-report --output text --query Content | base64 -d | head -1` confirms root password / access-key state, MFA device type and last-used dates. The hardware MFA token type (YubiKey / U2F / virtual) and physical vault location are recorded. Access-key presence on root is flagged as a critical finding. Evidence: redacted credential-report line for root, vault register photo, MFA device serial.
TC2. With explicit CISO pre-approval and the bank's root-custodian on the bridge, the tester observes ONE controlled root sign-in performed by the bank custodian during the test window while the tester measures detection: Sentinel rule `AWSCloudTrail | where userIdentity_type == 'Root' | where eventName == 'ConsoleLogin'`, Security Hub finding `IAM.7 Root user activity` and the SOC P1 incident creation. Evidence: CloudTrail console-login event ID, Sentinel incident ID, SOC acknowledgement timestamp."
AWS-003	AWS	Guardrails are the bank-wide automated 'no' for risky cloud configurations.	"TC1. The tester runs `aws controltower list-enabled-controls --target-identifier <ou-arn>` per OU plus `aws organizations list-policies --filter SERVICE_CONTROL_POLICY` to enumerate guardrail status across the organisation. The Landing Zone Accelerator deployment is also inspected via the configuration repo. Detective and preventive guardrails are mapped to each OU. Evidence: guardrail-by-OU CSV, LZA config snapshot, finding spreadsheet for OUs missing critical guardrails (e.g., 'Disallow S3 BPA disable').
TC2. From the tester role inside a non-prod member account, the tester attempts an action that a guardrail should block (e.g., `aws cloudtrail stop-logging --name <trail>` or `aws s3api put-public-access-block --bucket <bucket> --public-access-block-configuration BlockPublicAcls=false,...`). The action is expected to be denied at SCP / Config evaluation. Evidence: CLI denial output, CloudTrail event ID showing AccessDenied, Security Hub finding (if generated)."
AWS-004	AWS	SCP gaps allow privilege escalation that IAM controls alone cannot stop.	"TC1. The tester runs `aws organizations list-policies --filter SERVICE_CONTROL_POLICY` and `aws organizations list-targets-for-policy --policy-id <id>` to enumerate SCP attachments at root, OU and account level. Each SCP's JSON is downloaded via `aws organizations describe-policy --policy-id <id>` and analysed with PMapper / Iann0036's `aws-iam-policy-analyzer` to compute the effective deny set per account. Evidence: SCP-attachment matrix, effective-deny report per scoped account, finding spreadsheet for SCP gaps.
TC2. From the tester role inside an in-scope member account, the tester drives a per-service deny-probe (e.g., `aws lambda list-functions` if Lambda is denied at SCP, `aws kms list-keys` if KMS is restricted, `aws ec2 run-instances --dry-run` in a denied region). Each denied call's CloudTrail event is captured. Evidence: per-service CLI denial output, per-service CloudTrail event IDs, finding spreadsheet for any service that SCP claims to deny but actually allows."
AWS-005	AWS	Unused but unlocked regions become silent regions for adversary persistence.	"TC1. The tester enumerates region enablement per account via `aws account list-regions --account-id <id>` and the active SCP `aws:RequestedRegion` deny list, then cross-walks against actual resource presence via Steampipe `aws_region` table joined to `aws_ec2_instance`, `aws_s3_bucket`, etc. Regions enabled but unused are flagged as silent-persistence candidates. Evidence: region-enablement matrix CSV, regions-vs-usage gap report.
TC2. From the tester role, the tester attempts `aws ec2 run-instances --region <non-approved-region> --image-id <ami> --instance-type t3.nano --dry-run` and `aws s3api create-bucket --region <non-approved-region> --bucket nst-pentest-region-probe-$RANDOM`. SCP `aws:RequestedRegion` deny is expected to fire. Evidence: CLI denial output, CloudTrail event ID, finding spreadsheet for any region without enforcement."
AWS-006	AWS	Adversary path to spin up an account inside the organisation enables silent persistence.	"TC1. The tester enumerates account-provisioning paths: Control Tower Account Factory configuration in Service Catalog, Account Factory for Terraform repo, manual `aws organizations create-account` callers (CloudTrail lookup `aws cloudtrail lookup-events --lookup-attributes AttributeKey=EventName,AttributeValue=CreateAccount`). The principals authorised to create accounts and the approval gates (Service Catalog launch constraint, PR review) are mapped. Evidence: provisioning-path inventory, authorised-principal list, approval-gate diagram.
TC2. From a non-platform-team tester identity, the tester attempts `aws organizations create-account --email nst-pentest-probe@example.com --account-name 'nst-pentest-probe'`. The action is expected to be denied by SCP or IAM policy. Evidence: CLI denial output, CloudTrail event ID for CreateAccount AccessDenied, finding spreadsheet."
AWS-007	AWS	Long-lived access keys remain the single most common cloud breach vector.	"TC1. The tester runs `aws iam list-users`, `aws iam list-access-keys --user-name <u>` and `aws iam get-credential-report --output text --query Content | base64 -d` to build the IAM user and access-key inventory per scoped account. CloudFox `iam` module and Prowler check `iam_user_accesskey_unused` supplement this. Users with multiple keys, keys >90 days old or unused for >30 days are flagged. Evidence: full credential report (redacted), CloudFox CSV, Prowler JSON, finding spreadsheet.
TC2. The tester plants a canary IAM access key on a tester-owned IAM user (`nst-pentest-canary`) via `aws iam create-access-key`, embeds it in a public-facing test repo or paste-bin for 60 seconds, then revokes the key with `aws iam update-access-key --status Inactive` and `aws iam delete-access-key`. The tester monitors CloudTrail for any external use of the canary in the test window. Evidence: canary access-key ID, GuardDuty `UnauthorizedAccess:IAMUser/InstanceCredentialExfiltration.InsideAWS` finding (if any), CloudTrail event IDs for create / use / delete."
AWS-008	AWS	Keys older than the rotation window expand the time-window for credential leakage exploitation.	TC1. The tester pulls `aws iam get-credential-report --output text --query Content | base64 -d` per scoped account, parses access-key creation and last-used dates, and reports any key older than the bank's documented rotation policy threshold (typically 90 days). CloudFox `iam` and Steampipe `aws_iam_access_key` are used as cross-check. Evidence: aged-key CSV per account, policy-vs-actual gap report, finding spreadsheet.
AWS-009	AWS	Privileged role assumption is the core privilege-escalation test surface.	"TC1. The tester runs `aws iam list-roles` plus `aws iam list-attached-role-policies` for every role and filters for principals with `AdministratorAccess` or inline policies containing `""Action"": ""*""`. PMapper `graph create` builds the reachability graph and Pacu `iam__enum_permissions` records each role's effective ceiling. Trust policies are exported per privileged role with `aws iam get-role`. Evidence: privileged-role list per account, trust-policy JSON per role, PMapper graph DB.
TC2. From the tester role, the tester enumerates assumable roles via Pacu module `iam__privesc_scan` plus `aws sts assume-role --role-arn <arn> --role-session-name nst-pentest-chain` for each candidate. AssumeRole chains stop at the first denial; successful chains are logged with the role-arn pivot path. Evidence: PMapper chain output, sts assume-role transcript per role, CloudTrail event IDs for every AssumeRole call."
AWS-010	AWS	Without boundaries, an attached managed policy can grant more than intended.	"TC1. The tester runs `aws iam list-users --query 'Users[].PermissionsBoundary'` and `aws iam list-roles --query 'Roles[].PermissionsBoundary'` to inventory permission-boundary attachments. The boundary policy JSON is downloaded via `aws iam get-policy-version` and analysed with PMapper to compute the effective ceiling per principal. Principals without a boundary that the bank policy says should have one are flagged. Evidence: boundary-attachment matrix, effective-ceiling report per principal, finding spreadsheet.
TC2. Emulating T1548 Abuse Elevation Control Mechanism, the tester uses an authorised mid-privilege role to call `aws iam attach-user-policy --user-name nst-pentest-tester --policy-arn arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/AdministratorAccess` and immediately tests effective permissions with `aws iam simulate-principal-policy` plus a deliberately privileged action such as `aws iam create-user --user-name boundary-probe`. A correctly configured boundary should permit the attach but deny the elevated action. The tester then calls `aws iam detach-user-policy` within 60 seconds. Evidence: CloudTrail AttachUserPolicy / DetachUserPolicy event IDs, SimulatePrincipalPolicy output before and after, Security Hub IAM policy-change finding."
AWS-011	AWS	Wildcard trusts permit cross-account takeover from any AWS principal.	"TC1. The tester exports every IAM role trust policy via `aws iam list-roles --query 'Roles[].AssumeRolePolicyDocument'` and greps for `""Principal"": {""AWS"": ""*""}`, `""arn:aws:iam::*:root""` and missing `sts:ExternalId` conditions. Steampipe `aws_iam_role` and CloudFox `role-trusts` confirm. Findings are categorised by whether the wildcard is conditioned on org-ID, tag or fully open. Evidence: trust-policy CSV, wildcard-finding spreadsheet per account.
TC2. From a tester-owned AWS account outside the bank organisation, the tester attempts `aws sts assume-role --role-arn arn:aws:iam::<target-account>:role/<wildcarded-role> --role-session-name nst-pentest-cross-account-probe`. CloudTrail captures the attempt in both source and target accounts. If the assume-role succeeds the tester uses the resulting credentials only for `sts:GetCallerIdentity` and `iam:GetUser` to confirm the foothold, then immediately discards the session. Evidence: source / target CloudTrail event IDs, sts response credentials (redacted), finding spreadsheet."
AWS-012	AWS	Access Analyzer surfaces exactly the resource exposures an attacker would search for.	TC1. The tester runs `aws accessanalyzer list-analyzers` and `aws accessanalyzer list-findings --analyzer-arn <arn> --filter '{"status":{"eq":["ACTIVE"]}}'` to enumerate external-access and unused-access findings per account, including the bank's analyzer triage state. Findings older than 30 days without triage are flagged. Evidence: per-account Access Analyzer findings JSON, triage-age spreadsheet.
AWS-013	AWS	PassRole combined with service-create permissions equals one-shot privilege escalation.	"TC1. The tester runs PMapper `graph query 'who_can_pass_role <role-arn>'` plus a custom IAM-policy scan via `aws iam list-policies --scope Local --query 'Policies[]'` filtered for `iam:PassRole` without a resource constraint. CloudFox `permissions` module surfaces every principal with broad PassRole. Each finding is mapped to the services the role can be passed to (lambda.amazonaws.com, ec2.amazonaws.com, ecs-tasks.amazonaws.com, glue.amazonaws.com). Evidence: PMapper query output, broad-PassRole CSV, principal-to-service map.
TC2. Emulating T1548 via PassRole, the tester creates a tester Lambda function `nst-pentest-passrole-probe` with `aws lambda create-function --role <privileged-role-arn>` then triggers a single invocation that calls `sts:GetCallerIdentity` and returns the result. The function is deleted with `aws lambda delete-function` within 60 seconds. Evidence: CloudTrail PassRole / CreateFunction / Invoke / DeleteFunction event IDs, Lambda log line with the assumed identity, finding spreadsheet."
AWS-014	AWS	Inline policies escape central governance tooling and audit.	TC1. The tester enumerates inline policies via `aws iam list-user-policies`, `aws iam list-role-policies`, `aws iam list-group-policies` and pulls each via `aws iam get-user-policy / get-role-policy`. Counts per identity are compared against managed-policy attachments. Steampipe `aws_iam_policy_simulator` is used to assess whether each inline policy grants privileges that a managed policy already covers (redundant) or extends beyond (drift). Evidence: inline policy inventory CSV, redundant-vs-extending classification.
AWS-015	AWS	SLR abuse is rare but high-impact when present.	TC1. The tester enumerates service-linked roles via `aws iam list-roles --query 'Roles[?contains(Path, "/aws-service-role/")]'` and checks each for non-default attached policies via `aws iam list-attached-role-policies`. Any SLR with attached customer-managed policies or inline policies beyond the AWS-defined defaults is flagged for review. Evidence: SLR inventory CSV, non-default attachment list.
AWS-016	AWS	Identity Center compromise translates to multi-account compromise across the organisation.	"TC1. The tester enumerates IAM Identity Center via `aws sso-admin list-instances`, `aws sso-admin list-permission-sets --instance-arn <arn>`, `aws sso-admin describe-permission-set` and `aws sso-admin list-account-assignments`. The upstream IdP (Entra ID, Okta, or built-in) and the group-to-permission-set mapping are recorded. Permission sets with `AdministratorAccess` are highlighted. Evidence: permission-set inventory JSON, assignment-by-account matrix, finding spreadsheet.
TC2. From the tester user in the upstream IdP, the tester signs in to the AWS access portal and enumerates the assumable permission sets via `aws sso list-accounts` and `aws sso list-account-roles --access-token <token>`. Any permission set beyond the documented assignment is flagged. The tester does NOT assume any role beyond the documented one. Evidence: aws sso CLI output, access-portal screenshot, finding spreadsheet."
AWS-017	AWS	Shadow IdPs are persistence vectors that survive Identity Center rollouts.	TC1. Per scoped account the tester runs `aws iam list-saml-providers` and `aws iam list-open-id-connect-providers` and exports each via `aws iam get-saml-provider --saml-provider-arn <arn>`. The IdP metadata, signing cert fingerprint and any trusted role using each provider are mapped. Legacy IdPs that the bank claims to have decommissioned remain a finding even if unused (shadow IdP). Evidence: SAML / OIDC provider inventory CSV per account, signing-cert fingerprints, finding spreadsheet for shadow IdPs.
AWS-018	AWS	Mis-scoped web-identity trust grants direct cloud access to anyone with a social account.	TC1. The tester enumerates Cognito User Pools and Identity Pools via `aws cognito-idp list-user-pools --max-results 60`, `aws cognito-identity list-identity-pools --max-results 60` and `aws cognito-identity get-identity-pool-roles --identity-pool-id <id>`. Federated providers (google, facebook, login.microsoftonline.com) per identity pool plus the authenticated / unauthenticated role mappings are recorded. Identity pools granting authenticated role to any social provider are flagged. Evidence: Cognito inventory JSON, identity-pool role-mapping table.
AWS-019	AWS	Wildcarded OIDC trust to a CI provider equals full cloud takeover from any repo.	"TC1. The tester runs `aws iam list-open-id-connect-providers` and for each provider greps every role's trust policy for the matching audience and subject condition. Conditions like `""token.actions.githubusercontent.com:sub"": ""repo:bank-org/*""` (wildcarded) are flagged as findings, vs the secure `""repo:bank-org/payments-svc:ref:refs/heads/main""` form. Evidence: per-role trust-policy JSON, wildcarded-subject finding spreadsheet.
TC2. From a tester-controlled GitHub repository (`nst-pentest-org/probe`) the tester runs a workflow that calls `aws-actions/configure-aws-credentials` to attempt `sts:AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity` against the wildcarded role. If the assume succeeds the tester immediately calls `aws sts get-caller-identity` only and discards the session. Evidence: workflow run log, sts response (redacted), CloudTrail event ID for AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity, finding spreadsheet."
AWS-020	AWS	Defines emulation footprint and possible workload-identity escalation surface.	TC1. The tester pulls EC2 inventory via `aws ec2 describe-instances --query 'Reservations[].Instances[].[InstanceId, PublicIpAddress, MetadataOptions.HttpTokens, ImageId, IamInstanceProfile.Arn, Tags]'` plus Steampipe `aws_ec2_instance` for cross-region aggregation. Each instance row records OS, public-IP state, IMDSv2-only flag, attached IAM profile and patch-baseline state via SSM `aws ssm describe-instance-information`. Evidence: full EC2 inventory CSV per account, posture-flag summary, finding spreadsheet for high-risk instances.
AWS-021	AWS	IMDSv1 enables SSRF-style credential theft via any web vulnerability on the instance.	"TC1. The tester reads `MetadataOptions.HttpTokens` from `aws ec2 describe-instances` per instance and groups by `required` (IMDSv2-only), `optional` (IMDSv1 fallback) and missing. Prowler check `ec2_instance_imdsv2_enabled` and Steampipe `aws_ec2_instance` confirm. Counts and percentages per account / VPC are reported. Evidence: IMDS-state CSV per account, prowler JSON, finding spreadsheet for IMDSv1-fallback instances.
TC2. On a tester-owned EC2 instance configured to allow IMDSv1, the tester emulates T1552.005 by running `curl http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/iam/security-credentials/` then `curl http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/iam/security-credentials/<role>` to harvest the role's temporary credentials. The credentials are then used for a single `sts:GetCallerIdentity` call from outside the instance to demonstrate exfil viability. Evidence: curl output (redacted), CloudTrail event ID for the off-instance use, GuardDuty `UnauthorizedAccess:IAMUser/InstanceCredentialExfiltration.OutsideAWS` finding."
AWS-022	AWS	Over-privileged instance profile turns one SSRF into full account access.	"TC1. The tester enumerates instance profiles via `aws iam list-instance-profiles` and for each maps the attached role policies (managed + inline) with PMapper. Profiles whose roles grant `*:*`, `iam:*`, `kms:Decrypt` without resource constraint, or `AdministratorAccess` are flagged. Evidence: instance-profile inventory CSV, role-policy graph, finding spreadsheet for over-privileged profiles.
TC2. From the tester-owned EC2 instance, the tester uses the harvested instance-role credentials to call `aws s3api list-buckets`, `aws iam list-users`, `aws ec2 describe-instances --region <r>` and `aws kms list-keys` to demonstrate the blast radius of an SSRF-equivalent compromise. All calls are read-only; no resources are touched. Evidence: CLI output per call (redacted), CloudTrail event IDs, finding spreadsheet showing blast radius vs documented intent."
AWS-023	AWS	User-data is readable from metadata and frequently contains hard-coded credentials.	TC1. The tester pulls user-data per instance via `aws ec2 describe-instance-attribute --instance-id <id> --attribute userData --query 'UserData.Value' --output text | base64 -d` and greps for credential patterns (AKIA, eyJ, BEGIN PRIVATE KEY, password=, secret=, api_key=). Trufflehog is run over the decoded text as cross-check. Evidence: per-instance user-data hash, finding spreadsheet of secrets discovered, rotation tickets raised for any live credential.
AWS-024	AWS	SSM with logging gives forensic-grade visibility into interactive admin sessions.	"TC1. The tester runs `aws ssm describe-instance-information` to confirm SSM enrolment per instance, `aws ssm get-document --name SSM-SessionManagerRunShell` to read the session-preference document, and confirms logging destination (CloudWatch Logs / S3) plus KMS encryption. SGs are checked for port 22 / 3389 closure via `aws ec2 describe-security-groups`. Evidence: SSM enrolment CSV, session-preference JSON, SG audit, finding spreadsheet for instances with open SSH alongside SSM.
TC2. The tester starts a single `aws ssm start-session --target <tester-instance-id>` against a tester-owned EC2 instance, executes `hostname && date && echo NST-PENTEST-2026-Q2`, exits, then verifies the session manifest reaches the configured destination (CloudWatch Logs `/aws/ssm/SessionManager` or the S3 logs bucket) within target latency. Evidence: session-id, command transcript with watermark, CloudWatch Logs query screenshot, end-to-end latency value."
AWS-025	AWS	Public IPs expand attack surface to the internet and shorten time-to-compromise.	TC1. The tester pulls public-IP instances via `aws ec2 describe-instances --filters Name=ip-address,Values='*'` and `aws ec2 describe-network-interfaces --filters Name=association.public-ip,Values='*'`, then runs an authorised TCP/443 + TCP/80 banner-grab via masscan / nmap `-sV --script default` against each public IP (only with bank approval and inside the test window). Service banners and TLS certs are recorded. Evidence: public-IP inventory, nmap output, finding spreadsheet for unexpected services.
AWS-026	AWS	Public snapshots have repeatedly leaked credentials and customer data.	TC1. The tester runs `aws ec2 describe-snapshots --owner-ids self --query 'Snapshots[?StartTime>=\`2023-01-01\`]'` plus `aws ec2 describe-snapshot-attribute --snapshot-id <id> --attribute createVolumePermission` to enumerate snapshot ACLs and any cross-account shares including the public group `all`. Steampipe `aws_ebs_snapshot` confirms. Snapshots shared publicly or to non-bank accounts are flagged for immediate review. Evidence: snapshot ACL inventory, cross-account share matrix, finding spreadsheet.
AWS-027	AWS	Backdoored or modified public AMIs are a documented supply-chain vector.	TC1. The tester reads `aws ec2 describe-instances --query 'Reservations[].Instances[].ImageId'` per account then `aws ec2 describe-images --image-ids <id> --query 'Images[].[OwnerId, Public, Name]'` to classify each AMI as approved-baseline (bank account ID), AWS Marketplace, or unknown public. Public AMIs from non-bank owners are flagged. Evidence: AMI provenance CSV per running instance, public-AMI finding spreadsheet.
AWS-028	AWS	Deprecated runtimes carry known CVEs and miss security telemetry features.	TC1. The tester runs `aws lambda list-functions --query 'Functions[].[FunctionName, Runtime, Role, LastModified]'` per region per account and aggregates runtime distribution. Functions on AWS-deprecated runtimes (nodejs14.x, python3.7, etc.) are flagged for upgrade. CloudFox `lambda` module surfaces cross-account information. Evidence: Lambda inventory CSV per account, runtime-distribution histogram, deprecated-runtime finding spreadsheet.
AWS-029	AWS	Function compromise inherits the execution role's privileges directly.	"TC1. The tester pulls each function's execution role via `aws lambda get-function-configuration --function-name <fn> --query Role` and analyses the attached policies with PMapper / IAM policy simulator. Any role with `AdministratorAccess`, `*:*` or `iam:PassRole` without resource constraint is flagged. Evidence: per-function role-policy CSV, over-privileged-role finding spreadsheet.
TC2. With pre-approval, the tester deploys a tester Lambda `nst-pentest-execrole-probe` using `aws lambda create-function` with a permitted execution role and invokes it once via `aws lambda invoke`. The function code reads its role's effective permissions via `iam:GetRole / GetRolePolicy` and writes results to CloudWatch Logs. The function is deleted within 5 minutes via `aws lambda delete-function`. Evidence: function ARN, invocation request-id, CloudWatch Logs output, CreateFunction / Invoke / DeleteFunction CloudTrail events."
AWS-030	AWS	Env vars are readable by anyone with GetFunctionConfiguration permission.	TC1. The tester runs `aws lambda get-function-configuration --function-name <fn> --query 'Environment.Variables'` per function and pipes the output through Trufflehog regex patterns and Gitleaks rules for secrets (AKIA*, eyJ*, slack tokens, db URIs containing credentials). Functions with embedded secrets are flagged for migration to Secrets Manager / Parameter Store. Evidence: env-var inventory per function, secrets-finding spreadsheet, rotation tickets for live credentials.
AWS-031	AWS	Wildcard resource policy creates an unauthenticated invocation pathway.	TC1. The tester reads each function's resource policy via `aws lambda get-policy --function-name <fn>` and greps for `"Principal": "*"`, `"Principal": {"AWS": "*"}` or `lambda:InvokeFunction` without source-arn / source-account condition. Public-invokable functions are flagged as findings. Evidence: per-function resource-policy JSON, public-invoke finding spreadsheet.
AWS-032	AWS	Layer backdooring is a documented persistence technique difficult to spot in deployments.	TC1. The tester enumerates layers per function via `aws lambda list-functions --query 'Functions[].Layers'` plus `aws lambda get-layer-version` to identify the layer source ARN. Layers from accounts outside the bank organisation (public marketplace, third-party developer) are flagged for supply-chain risk. Evidence: layer-source CSV per function, third-party-layer finding spreadsheet.
AWS-033	AWS	Authorizer bypass equals direct unauthenticated backend access.	"TC1. The tester enumerates API Gateway via `aws apigateway get-rest-apis`, `aws apigatewayv2 get-apis` and per-API `aws apigateway get-resources` + `get-method` to extract the authorizer type (NONE, IAM, Lambda, Cognito, JWT). Endpoints with `AuthorizationType=NONE` on non-public-by-design paths are flagged. Evidence: endpoint inventory CSV, authorizer-type histogram, finding spreadsheet.
TC2. Against each public endpoint, the tester sends a single unauthenticated `curl -i -X GET https://<api-id>.execute-api.<region>.amazonaws.com/<stage>/<resource>` with the watermark in the User-Agent. Rejection (401 / 403) is expected. Endpoints that return 200 to unauthenticated calls are flagged as findings. Evidence: per-endpoint HTTP request / response capture, finding spreadsheet, WAF / CloudWatch metrics for the request."
AWS-034	AWS	SFN log verbosity directly affects visibility into orchestrated abuse.	TC1. The tester runs `aws stepfunctions list-state-machines` plus `describe-state-machine --state-machine-arn <arn>` to pull definition, role arn and `loggingConfiguration.level` per state machine. State machines with cross-account roles in trust policy, `iam:PassRole` to other services, or `loggingConfiguration.level=OFF` are flagged. Evidence: state-machine inventory CSV, role-and-logging matrix, finding spreadsheet.
AWS-035	AWS	EventBridge poisoning enables function trigger abuse from outside the account.	"TC1. The tester enumerates EventBridge buses via `aws events list-event-buses` and per-bus `aws events describe-event-bus --name <bus>` to read the resource policy plus `aws events list-rules` for rules. Buses with wildcarded `Principal` or cross-account grants outside the bank org are flagged. Evidence: per-bus resource policy JSON, cross-account-grant matrix, rule inventory, finding spreadsheet.
TC2. From a tester-controlled AWS account outside the bank organisation, the tester sends a single benign canary event via `aws events put-events --entries '[{""Source"":""nst.pentest"",""DetailType"":""canary"",""Detail"":""{\""watermark\"":\""NST-PENTEST-2026-Q2\""}"",""EventBusName"":""<bus-arn>""}]'`. The tester then queries the SIEM for the event and confirms whether a rule fires alerting on unexpected source. Evidence: PutEvents API output, SIEM query result, finding spreadsheet for missing alerts."
AWS-036	AWS	Public EKS API endpoint expands attack surface to anyone with a valid token.	TC1. The tester runs `aws eks list-clusters` and per-cluster `aws eks describe-cluster --name <c> --query 'cluster.[name, version, resourcesVpcConfig.endpointPublicAccess, resourcesVpcConfig.publicAccessCidrs, resourcesVpcConfig.endpointPrivateAccess]'` to capture EKS inventory, K8s version and endpoint posture. Clusters with `endpointPublicAccess=true` and `publicAccessCidrs=['0.0.0.0/0']` are critical findings. Evidence: EKS inventory CSV, endpoint-posture matrix, finding spreadsheet.
AWS-037	AWS	Pods sharing the node role inherit unrelated permissions and break blast-radius controls.	"TC1. The tester runs `kubectl get sa --all-namespaces -o json | jq '.items[] | {ns:.metadata.namespace, name:.metadata.name, role:.metadata.annotations.""eks.amazonaws.com/role-arn""}'` to dump every IRSA mapping plus `aws eks list-pod-identity-associations --cluster-name <c>` for Pod Identity. Pods running with the node instance profile (no SA annotation) are flagged as falling back to node privilege. Evidence: IRSA / Pod Identity mapping CSV, fallback-pod finding spreadsheet.
TC2. From a tester-controlled pod scheduled into a sandbox namespace, the tester exec's `curl http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/iam/security-credentials/` to attempt to read node-instance-profile credentials. A correctly configured cluster (with IMDS hop-limit 1, blocked at network policy) denies the request. Evidence: pod exec transcript, network-policy denial log, finding spreadsheet for clusters that allow the call."
AWS-038	AWS	K8s RBAC is the pre-AWS layer for any adversary inside a cluster.	"TC1. The tester runs `kubectl get clusterrole,clusterrolebinding -o json | jq` and uses `rbac-lookup`, `rbac-tool`, `kubescape scan framework nsa` and `kube-bench` to enumerate cluster-admin bindings, default-service-account usage and any anonymous-auth (`system:anonymous` / `system:unauthenticated`) bindings. Evidence: RBAC inventory JSON, kubescape report, anonymous-auth finding spreadsheet.
TC2. From a tester-controlled pod, the tester runs `kube-hunter --pod`, `kubescape scan` and BloodHound for K8s queries to enumerate RBAC paths and reachable resources from the pod's service account. The tester does NOT exploit any escalation path beyond enumeration. Evidence: kube-hunter JSON, BloodHound graph screenshot, RBAC-path finding spreadsheet."
AWS-039	AWS	Privileged-pod prevention is the main control against container-to-host escape.	"TC1. The tester enumerates admission controllers via `kubectl get validatingwebhookconfiguration,mutatingwebhookconfiguration -o json` and Kyverno `kubectl get cpol,pol --all-namespaces` and OPA Gatekeeper `kubectl get constraints --all-namespaces`. Pod Security Standards enforcement is checked at namespace level via `kubectl get ns -o jsonpath='{.items[*].metadata.labels.pod-security\.kubernetes\.io/enforce}'`. Evidence: admission inventory CSV, enforcement-mode matrix, finding spreadsheet for namespaces without enforcement.
TC2. From the tester's sandbox namespace, the tester applies a manifest creating a privileged pod (`securityContext.privileged: true`, `hostPID: true`, `hostNetwork: true`) and confirms admission denial. Evidence: kubectl apply error message showing admission denial, Kyverno / Gatekeeper audit log entry, finding spreadsheet for namespaces that allow privileged deploy."
AWS-040	AWS	Task-role abuse parallels Lambda execution-role abuse.	TC1. The tester runs `aws ecs list-task-definitions` and per-task-definition `aws ecs describe-task-definition --task-definition <td> --query 'taskDefinition.taskRoleArn'` to inventory ECS task roles. Each task role's effective ceiling is computed via PMapper; over-privileged task roles are flagged. Evidence: ECS task-definition inventory CSV, task-role policy matrix, over-privileged finding spreadsheet.
AWS-041	AWS	Fargate excludes node-level container-escape attacks and changes the test approach.	TC1. The tester runs `aws ecs list-services --cluster <c>` plus `aws ecs describe-services --cluster <c> --services <s> --query 'services[].launchType'` and aggregates Fargate vs EC2 counts per cluster. The launch-type mix informs whether node-level container-escape techniques are even applicable (Fargate isolates from host). Evidence: launch-type breakdown CSV, per-cluster Fargate / EC2 ratio.
AWS-042	AWS	Stale images carry vulnerable libraries that re-introduce known CVEs into production.	TC1. The tester runs `aws ecr describe-repositories` per region per account, `aws ecr get-lifecycle-policy --repository-name <r>` and `aws ecr describe-image-scan-findings --repository-name <r> --image-id imageTag=latest`. Repositories without lifecycle policy or with critical / high CVE counts above threshold are flagged. Evidence: ECR inventory CSV, lifecycle-policy state, scan-finding summary per repo.
AWS-043	AWS	Unsigned image push to a trusted registry equals supply-chain compromise.	"TC1. The tester verifies image-signing enforcement by reading the Kyverno / Connaisseur / Sigstore policy in cluster (`kubectl get cpol | grep verifyImages`), the ECR signing-profile (`aws signer list-signing-profiles`) and the ACR Content Trust state. Sample image is verified with `cosign verify --certificate-identity-regexp '.+@bank.example' --certificate-oidc-issuer https://accounts.google.com <image>`. Evidence: signing-policy YAML, signer profile JSON, cosign verify output per sample, finding spreadsheet for clusters without enforcement.
TC2. The tester builds and pushes an unsigned image `nst-pentest-unsigned:<watermark>` to a tester sandbox registry path then applies a `Deployment` manifest pointing at the unsigned image to a sandbox namespace on the in-scope cluster. Admission is expected to reject the deploy via Kyverno verifyImages / Connaisseur. Evidence: kubectl apply error message, admission audit log, finding spreadsheet for clusters that accept unsigned."
AWS-044	AWS	Bucket exposure remains the most-reported cloud breach class.	TC1. The tester runs `aws s3api list-buckets` per account, `aws s3api get-bucket-tagging --bucket <b>` for classification tags, and joins with Macie data-classification findings where available. Buckets without classification tags are flagged for the data-governance team. CloudFox `aws inventory` and Steampipe `aws_s3_bucket` confirm. Evidence: S3 inventory CSV per account, classification-tag matrix, untagged-bucket finding spreadsheet.
AWS-045	AWS	Account-level BPA prevents accidental public exposure across the whole account.	"TC1. The tester runs `aws s3control get-public-access-block --account-id <id>` and per-bucket `aws s3api get-public-access-block --bucket <b>` to confirm BPA state at account and bucket level. Prowler check `s3_account_level_public_access_blocks` and Steampipe `aws_s3_account_settings` cross-check. Accounts or buckets with any of the four BPA flags off are flagged. Evidence: BPA state per account and bucket, finding spreadsheet for exceptions.
TC2. On a tester-owned non-prod S3 bucket `nst-pentest-bpa-probe-<random>`, the tester disables BPA via `aws s3api put-public-access-block --bucket <b> --public-access-block-configuration 'BlockPublicAcls=false,IgnorePublicAcls=false,BlockPublicPolicy=false,RestrictPublicBuckets=false'`, then immediately re-enables it within 60 seconds. The tester confirms Sentinel / Security Hub alert fires (`AWSCloudTrail | where eventName == 'PutBucketPublicAccessBlock'`). Evidence: CloudTrail PutBucketPublicAccessBlock event IDs, alert ID, finding spreadsheet for missing alert."
AWS-046	AWS	Public bucket policies leak data the moment they are saved.	TC1. The tester runs `aws s3api get-bucket-policy --bucket <b>` and `aws s3api get-bucket-acl --bucket <b>` per bucket and parses for wildcarded `Principal` (`*` or `arn:aws:iam::*:root`), `Grantee.URI` matching `http://acs.amazonaws.com/groups/global/AllUsers` or `AuthenticatedUsers`. Prowler `s3_bucket_policy_public_write_access` and Steampipe queries confirm. Evidence: per-bucket policy / ACL JSON, public-principal finding spreadsheet.
AWS-047	AWS	Long-lived presigned URLs become persistent exfil channels even after credentials rotate.	TC1. The tester searches the in-scope source-code repositories with Grep / GitHub code-search for presigned-URL issuance patterns (`generate_presigned_url`, `presigned_post`, `s3.GetObjectInput`, `Sas...`) and inspects the configured TTL / Expires parameter. URLs issued with TTL >24 hours are flagged. A sample of recent presigned URLs from CloudWatch / CloudTrail data events is sampled for actual TTL distribution. Evidence: code-search grep results, TTL distribution histogram, finding spreadsheet for long-lived URL patterns.
AWS-048	AWS	Object Lock provides ransomware-overwrite resistance for crown-jewel data.	"TC1. The tester runs `aws s3api get-object-lock-configuration --bucket <b>` and `aws s3api get-bucket-versioning --bucket <b>` per bucket. Buckets storing crown-jewel data without Object Lock in COMPLIANCE mode or without versioning are flagged. Evidence: per-bucket configuration JSON, finding spreadsheet for crown-jewel buckets without lock / versioning.
TC2. On a tester-owned bucket with Object Lock COMPLIANCE mode and a locked object `nst-pentest-lock-probe.txt`, the tester attempts `aws s3api delete-object --bucket <b> --key nst-pentest-lock-probe.txt --version-id <v>` and `aws s3api put-object-retention --bucket <b> --key <k> --retention 'Mode=COMPLIANCE,RetainUntilDate=...'` to extend earlier. Delete should be denied. Evidence: CLI denial output, CloudTrail event ID, finding spreadsheet for buckets that allow delete."
AWS-049	AWS	Replication out of jurisdiction is both a data-residency breach and a covert exfil path.	TC1. The tester runs `aws s3api get-bucket-replication --bucket <b>` per bucket to enumerate CRR / SRR rules including destination bucket ARN, destination account and replicate-delete-markers state. Destination buckets in non-approved regions or outside the bank account list are flagged as residency / exfil findings. Evidence: per-bucket replication-config JSON, destination matrix, finding spreadsheet.
AWS-050	AWS	Macie reveals sensitive data sitting in buckets that should not hold it.	TC1. The tester runs `aws macie2 get-administrator-account`, `aws macie2 describe-buckets` and `aws macie2 list-findings --finding-criteria '{"criterion":{"severity.description":{"eq":["High"]}}}'` to determine Macie coverage and current high-severity sensitive-data findings. Buckets in scope but not under Macie classification jobs are flagged. Evidence: Macie coverage CSV, current high-severity findings list, gap spreadsheet.
AWS-051	AWS	Mount-target exposure enables direct NFS / SMB lateral movement.	TC1. The tester enumerates EFS via `aws efs describe-file-systems` and `aws efs describe-mount-targets --file-system-id <fs>` plus FSx `aws fsx describe-file-systems` and joins mount-target ENIs to security groups via `aws ec2 describe-security-groups --group-ids`. Mount targets with SGs allowing 0.0.0.0/0 on NFS port 2049 or SMB port 445 are critical findings. Evidence: mount-target inventory CSV, SG audit results, finding spreadsheet.
AWS-052	AWS	Public RDS exposes a primary attack surface directly to the internet.	TC1. The tester runs `aws rds describe-db-instances --query 'DBInstances[].[DBInstanceIdentifier, Engine, EngineVersion, PubliclyAccessible, Endpoint.Address]'` and the equivalent for Aurora clusters via `aws rds describe-db-clusters`. Instances with `PubliclyAccessible=true` are critical findings. Prowler check `rds_instance_no_public_access` confirms. Evidence: RDS inventory CSV per account, public-accessibility finding spreadsheet.
AWS-053	AWS	Customer-managed keys provide audit and revocation that AWS-managed keys do not.	TC1. The tester reads `aws rds describe-db-instances --query 'DBInstances[].[DBInstanceIdentifier, KmsKeyId, StorageEncrypted]'` and `aws kms describe-key --key-id <arn>` plus `aws kms get-key-rotation-status` to confirm encryption with customer-managed KMS keys per cluster. Clusters using the default AWS-managed `aws/rds` key or with rotation disabled are flagged. Evidence: per-cluster encryption matrix, KMS rotation-state, finding spreadsheet.
AWS-054	AWS	Token-based DB auth changes the credential-theft model meaningfully.	TC1. The tester runs `aws rds describe-db-instances --query 'DBInstances[?IAMDatabaseAuthenticationEnabled==\`true\`].DBInstanceIdentifier'` per region per account to enumerate IAM-auth DBs. For each, the bank's documented DB-user-to-IAM-role mapping is reviewed against actual DB grants. Evidence: IAM-auth DB list, user-mapping spreadsheet per engine, finding spreadsheet for inconsistent mappings.
AWS-055	AWS	Public DB snapshots have repeatedly leaked customer data.	TC1. The tester runs `aws rds describe-db-snapshots --snapshot-type manual --query 'DBSnapshots[].DBSnapshotIdentifier'` then `aws rds describe-db-snapshot-attributes --db-snapshot-identifier <id>` per snapshot to enumerate snapshot ACLs. Snapshots shared to `all` (public) or to non-bank account IDs are critical findings. The same is run for cluster snapshots. Evidence: snapshot ACL inventory CSV, public / cross-account share matrix.
AWS-056	AWS	Data API permits SQL via IAM, opening a different attack vector to network-level exposure.	TC1. The tester runs `aws rds describe-db-clusters --query 'DBClusters[?EnableHttpEndpoint==\`true\`].[DBClusterIdentifier, Engine, EngineMode]'` to enumerate Aurora Serverless v2 clusters with Data API enabled. For each, the IAM principals with `rds-data:ExecuteStatement` are pulled via PMapper. Evidence: Aurora Data API inventory, principal-with-rds-data permission spreadsheet, finding spreadsheet for over-privileged principals.
AWS-057	AWS	DynamoDB resource policies are recent (2024) and often under-audited.	TC1. The tester runs `aws dynamodb list-tables` and per-table `aws dynamodb describe-table --table-name <t>`, `aws dynamodb get-resource-policy --resource-arn <arn>` (2024-introduced) and `aws dynamodb describe-continuous-backups --table-name <t>`. Tables holding sensitive data without PITR or with wildcarded resource policy are flagged. Evidence: per-table config JSON, resource-policy audit, PITR state matrix, finding spreadsheet.
AWS-058	AWS	Redshift data shares are powerful exfil paths if granted across the wrong accounts.	TC1. The tester runs `aws redshift describe-clusters` and `aws redshift describe-data-shares --query 'DataShares[].[DataShareArn, ProducerArn, DataShareAssociations]'` to enumerate Redshift data-share producers and consumers. Consumer-account IDs are checked against the bank account list; external consumers are flagged as exfil-path findings. Evidence: data-share inventory CSV, consumer-account matrix, finding spreadsheet.
AWS-059	AWS	Topology defines lateral-movement paths and the speed at which an adversary can pivot.	TC1. The tester runs `aws ec2 describe-vpcs`, `aws ec2 describe-transit-gateways`, `aws ec2 describe-transit-gateway-route-tables` and `aws ec2 describe-route-tables` per account / region to build a complete VPC + TGW topology. Hava / Lucid / cloud-architect produced topology diagrams are saved as evidence. Routes leading to the internet via instances rather than IGW / NAT are flagged. Evidence: VPC / TGW JSON, topology diagrams, finding spreadsheet for unusual routes.
AWS-060	AWS	Public-subnet placement combined with weak SG is a recurring exposure pattern.	TC1. The tester enumerates public subnets by querying `aws ec2 describe-subnets` joined to `aws ec2 describe-route-tables` filtering for `gateway-id starts with igw-` then `aws ec2 describe-network-interfaces --filters Name=subnet-id,Values=<public-subnets>` to enumerate ENIs. Each ENI is mapped to its attached resource (EC2, RDS, ELB, Lambda). Evidence: public-subnet inventory CSV, contained-ENI matrix, finding spreadsheet for unexpected placements.
AWS-061	AWS	Open management ports turn opportunistic scans into actual compromise events.	"TC1. The tester runs `aws ec2 describe-security-groups --query 'SecurityGroups[].[GroupId, IpPermissions]'` and Steampipe `aws_vpc_security_group_rule` to find rules with CidrIp=0.0.0.0/0 and FromPort/ToPort overlapping 22, 3389, 1433, 3306, 5432, 27017, 6379, 9200. Prowler check `ec2_securitygroup_allow_ingress_from_internet_to_any_port` confirms. Evidence: SG audit CSV, exposed-port matrix, finding spreadsheet.
TC2. From the tester source IP (allow-listed inside the test window), the tester runs `nmap -sS -p 22,3389,1433,3306,5432,27017,6379,9200 --max-rate 100 <ip-list>` against the public IPs identified from the SG audit to confirm whether the port is actually reachable. Evidence: nmap output per target, screenshot of open-port banner where applicable."
AWS-062	AWS	Flow logs are the only network-layer evidence in cloud forensics.	TC1. The tester runs `aws ec2 describe-flow-logs --query 'FlowLogs[].[ResourceId, LogDestination, LogDestinationType, TrafficType, FlowLogStatus]'` per region per account and joins with `aws ec2 describe-vpcs` to identify any VPC without a Flow Log or with ACCEPT-only traffic captured. Destination type (S3, CloudWatch Logs, Kinesis Firehose) is recorded. Evidence: Flow-Log coverage CSV, missing-VPC list, finding spreadsheet.
AWS-063	AWS	Endpoint-policy bypass enables external accounts to use your network for their resources.	TC1. The tester runs `aws ec2 describe-vpc-endpoints` per region and per endpoint `--query 'VpcEndpoints[].[VpcEndpointId, ServiceName, PolicyDocument]'`. Each PolicyDocument is parsed for missing `aws:PrincipalOrgID`, `aws:ResourceOrgID` and `aws:SourceVpc` conditions. Endpoint policies allowing `"*"` Principal without org-scoping are flagged as confused-deputy risk. Evidence: per-endpoint policy JSON, condition-key audit, finding spreadsheet.
AWS-064	AWS	On-prem trusts become bidirectional pivot paths during an incident.	TC1. The tester runs `aws directconnect describe-connections`, `aws directconnect describe-virtual-interfaces`, `aws ec2 describe-vpn-connections` and `aws ec2 describe-transit-gateway-route-tables` to enumerate Direct Connect and S2S VPN trusts plus BGP advertised / propagated routes. Routes advertised from on-prem that map to internet-routable CIDRs or non-bank ranges are flagged as policy violations or pivot risk. Evidence: DX / VPN inventory CSV, propagated-route matrix, finding spreadsheet.
AWS-065	AWS	Layer-7 protection baseline for public banking endpoints.	TC1. The tester enumerates WAF v2 via `aws wafv2 list-web-acls --scope REGIONAL` and `--scope CLOUDFRONT`, plus `aws wafv2 list-rule-groups`. For each Web ACL the rule sources (AWSManagedRulesCommonRuleSet, AWSManagedRulesSQLiRuleSet, custom rules) and recent block / count metrics from CloudWatch `WAFv2 BlockedRequests` are pulled. Shield Advanced subscription state is checked via `aws shield describe-subscription`. Evidence: WAF inventory CSV, rule-source matrix, recent block-rate dashboard screenshot.
AWS-066	AWS	Dangling DNS enables subdomain takeover and brand-impersonation attacks.	"TC1. The tester runs `aws route53 list-hosted-zones` and per zone `aws route53 list-resource-record-sets --hosted-zone-id <z>`. Each A / AAAA / CNAME / ALIAS record is resolved via `dig +short` and the target is checked against live cloud-resource inventory (S3, CloudFront, ELB, ElasticBeanstalk, API Gateway). Records pointing at IPs / hostnames that no longer resolve to a bank-owned resource are flagged as dangling. Tools: dnsReaper, subjack, can-i-take-over-xyz. Evidence: Route 53 record dump, dangling-target candidate list.
TC2. For each dangling-candidate record, the tester performs a non-claim probe: `curl -sI https://<dangling-cname>` and provider-specific bucket-existence checks (`aws s3api head-bucket --bucket <name>` for S3 dangling, response inspection for CloudFront / Heroku). The tester does NOT register the target. Evidence: per-candidate response capture, dangling-confirmed spreadsheet for bank action."
AWS-067	AWS	A single secrets store concentrates compromise risk and must be tightly controlled.	"TC1. The tester runs `aws secretsmanager list-secrets` and per-secret `aws secretsmanager describe-secret --secret-id <id>` plus `aws secretsmanager get-resource-policy --secret-id <id>` to inventory secrets, rotation lambdas, last-rotated date and resource policies. Secrets with `RotationEnabled=false` or `LastRotatedDate` older than the policy threshold are flagged; resource policies with `""Principal"": ""*""` are critical findings. Evidence: per-secret config CSV, rotation-state matrix, resource-policy audit.
TC2. From the tester low-priv role, the tester attempts `aws secretsmanager get-secret-value --secret-id <forbidden-secret>` against a secret the tester is documented as NOT entitled to. Denial is expected via resource policy or IAM. Evidence: CLI denial output, CloudTrail event ID for the GetSecretValue AccessDenied, finding spreadsheet for any unexpectedly readable secret."
AWS-068	AWS	Parameter Store is often forgotten in audits despite holding the same class of secret.	TC1. The tester runs `aws ssm describe-parameters --parameter-filters Key=Type,Values=SecureString` per region per account to enumerate SecureString parameters. Each is matched to the KMS key used and the IAM principals with `ssm:GetParameter` / `kms:Decrypt` on that key via PMapper. Evidence: SecureString parameter inventory CSV, KMS-key access matrix, finding spreadsheet for over-broad access.
AWS-069	AWS	KMS grant abuse lets an attacker decrypt arbitrary protected data.	TC1. The tester runs `aws kms list-keys` and per-key `aws kms get-key-policy --key-id <id> --policy-name default` plus `aws kms list-grants --key-id <id>`. Key policies with `"Principal": "*"` without condition keys (`kms:CallerAccount`, `kms:ViaService`) or broad `kms:CreateGrant` permissions are flagged. CloudFox `kms` and Prowler `kms_key_not_publicly_accessible` confirm. Evidence: KMS key-policy inventory JSON, grant-audit CSV, finding spreadsheet.
AWS-070	AWS	Cross-account decrypt paths are easy to grant and hard to revoke.	TC1. The tester runs `aws kms list-grants --key-id <id>` per key plus key-policy parsing for cross-account `Principal` entries. Each cross-account grant is mapped to the target AWS account ID, the granted operation set (`Decrypt`, `ReEncryptFrom`, `GenerateDataKey`) and the business use-case from the bank's key-governance register. Grants without documented use-case are flagged. Evidence: cross-account KMS grant matrix, use-case-vs-grant gap spreadsheet.
AWS-071	AWS	Payment-critical key store with the strongest controls available in AWS.	TC1. The tester runs `aws cloudhsmv2 describe-clusters` and inspects the cluster CO / CU quorum settings via the CloudHSM CLI (`cloudhsm_mgmt_util` `listUsers`). Audit-log destination is verified via `aws cloudhsmv2 describe-backups` and CloudWatch Logs `/aws/cloudhsm/<cluster>` group existence. Clusters with a single CO or no audit destination are flagged. Evidence: HSM cluster inventory CSV, quorum-config screenshot, audit-destination state.
AWS-072	AWS	CloudTrail is the single source of audit truth for AWS.	"TC1. The tester runs `aws cloudtrail describe-trails --include-shadow-trails` and per-trail `aws cloudtrail get-event-selectors --trail-name <t>` plus `aws cloudtrail get-trail-status --name <t>`. The Organisation Trail flag, multi-region flag, log file validation, KMS encryption and data-event selectors for S3 / Lambda / DynamoDB are verified. Accounts without organisation-trail coverage are flagged. Evidence: trail configuration CSV, data-event coverage matrix, finding spreadsheet.
TC2. On a tester-owned non-prod trail `nst-pentest-trail-probe` in a non-prod account, the tester calls `aws cloudtrail stop-logging --name <trail>` then `aws cloudtrail start-logging --name <trail>` within 30 seconds. The expected Sentinel / GuardDuty alert is `Stealth:IAMUser/CloudTrailLoggingDisabled` / Security Hub `CloudTrail.1`. Evidence: CloudTrail StopLogging / StartLogging event IDs, GuardDuty finding ID, SOC P1 incident reference."
AWS-073	AWS	Look-back depth determines how far past a breach can be investigated.	TC1. The tester runs `aws cloudtrail list-event-data-stores` and per-store `aws cloudtrail get-event-data-store --event-data-store <arn>` to confirm retention in days. A representative SQL query (`SELECT eventTime, eventName FROM <store> WHERE userIdentity.userName='nst-pentest-tester' LIMIT 10`) is run via `aws cloudtrail start-query` to confirm queryability. Evidence: event-data-store config CSV, sample query result, retention-policy gap spreadsheet.
AWS-074	AWS	Each protection plan covers a different attack class that would otherwise be silent.	"TC1. The tester runs `aws guardduty list-detectors` and per-detector `aws guardduty get-detector --detector-id <id>` plus `aws guardduty list-organization-admin-accounts` to enumerate GuardDuty deployment, master / member topology and feature-flag state for S3 Protection, EKS Protection, Malware Protection, RDS Login Protection, Lambda Protection. Accounts / regions without coverage are flagged. Evidence: detector inventory CSV, feature-coverage matrix, finding spreadsheet.
TC2. From a tester-owned EC2 instance, the tester runs the GuardDuty Tester scripts (`amazon-guardduty-tester` repo) for a curated subset: `Recon:EC2/PortProbeUnprotectedPort`, `UnauthorizedAccess:IAMUser/MaliciousIPCaller.Custom`, `CryptoCurrency:EC2/BitcoinTool.B!DNS`. Each triggered finding ID is captured, plus the SOC alert reference and SOAR run. Evidence: GuardDuty finding IDs, SOC incident IDs, SOAR run log, finding spreadsheet for missed detections."
AWS-075	AWS	Single pane that consolidates posture across all in-scope accounts.	TC1. The tester runs `aws securityhub get-enabled-standards` and `aws securityhub get-findings --filters '{"SeverityLabel":[{"Value":"HIGH","Comparison":"EQUALS"}],"WorkflowStatus":[{"Value":"NEW","Comparison":"EQUALS"}]}'` to enumerate enabled standards (CIS, AWS Foundational, PCI DSS) and high-severity open findings count. Accounts not enrolled in the org-aggregator are flagged. Evidence: standards-enabled CSV, high-severity finding summary, aggregator membership matrix.
AWS-076	AWS	Config gives the drift-and-compliance evidence trail auditors expect.	TC1. The tester runs `aws configservice describe-configuration-recorders` and `aws configservice describe-configuration-recorder-status` per region per account to verify all-region recording. `aws configservice describe-conformance-packs` plus `aws configservice describe-conformance-pack-compliance` summarise NIST / PCI / CIS pack results. Recorders stopped or missing data resources are flagged. Evidence: Config recorder status CSV, conformance pack compliance summary.
AWS-077	AWS	Detective accelerates post-incident reconstruction of identity activity.	TC1. The tester runs `aws detective list-graphs` and `aws detective list-members --graph-arn <arn>` to enumerate Detective behaviour-graph coverage and member-account enrolment. Accounts not enrolled or pending acceptance are flagged. Evidence: Detective graph membership matrix, enrolment-gap spreadsheet.
AWS-078	AWS	Pipeline failure at this junction means real-time detection silently stops.	"TC1. The tester documents the forwarding pipeline: CloudTrail -> S3 (with SQS notifications) -> Kinesis Firehose / Splunk Add-on for AWS / Sentinel Continuous Export -> SIEM. GuardDuty findings via EventBridge -> SOAR -> SIEM ingestion. VPC Flow Logs to CloudWatch / S3 -> SIEM. The last-event-received timestamp per source is read via SIEM query `AWSCloudTrail | summarize max(TimeGenerated) by AWSAccountId`. Evidence: pipeline architecture diagram, per-source latency table, finding spreadsheet for stale feeds.
TC2. From the tester federated identity the tester fires `aws sts get-caller-identity --query 'Account'` and `aws ec2 describe-regions` with the watermark in the User-Agent (`AWS_REQUEST_USER_AGENT_NOTE='NST-PENTEST-2026-Q2'`), then immediately runs a SIEM KQL / SPL query for the watermark to measure end-to-end latency from API call to indexed event. The latency is logged per source. Evidence: API call timestamp, SIEM query screenshot with timestamp, latency value."
AWS-079	AWS	These are the baseline must-have detections for AWS incidents.	"TC1. The tester enumerates SIEM rule inventory (Sentinel `Get-AzSentinelAlertRule`, Splunk `| rest /servicesNS/-/-/saved/searches`) and maps each existing rule to the baseline list (root login, console-without-MFA, IAM-AccessKey-create, admin-policy-attach, GuardDuty-High, CloudTrail-disabled, S3-BPA-disabled, IMDSv1-fallback). Baseline items missing a rule are flagged. Evidence: rule-to-baseline matrix CSV, missing-rule list.
TC2. From the tester federated identity, the tester fires each baseline scenario in sequence: a planned root sign-in (bank-owned, see Section AWS-Mgmt), `aws iam create-access-key` on a tester user, `aws iam attach-user-policy` for AdministratorAccess (then immediate detach), `aws cloudtrail stop-logging` on a tester trail, `aws s3api put-public-access-block` disable on a tester bucket. Each scenario's alert ID and time-to-detect are captured. Evidence: per-scenario CloudTrail event IDs, SIEM alert IDs, SOC acknowledgement timestamps, TTD measurements."
AWS-080	AWS	Adversaries delete backups before encrypting data; vault lock prevents this.	"TC1. The tester runs `aws backup list-backup-plans`, `aws backup list-backup-vaults` and per-vault `aws backup describe-backup-vault --backup-vault-name <v>` to enumerate plans, vaults and `Locked / LockDate / LockType` (governance vs compliance). Vaults without Vault Lock or with governance-mode only on crown-jewel data are flagged. Evidence: vault inventory CSV, lock-state matrix, finding spreadsheet.
TC2. On a tester-owned vault-locked (compliance mode) test vault `nst-pentest-vault-lock-probe`, the tester attempts `aws backup delete-recovery-point --backup-vault-name <v> --recovery-point-arn <arn>` and `aws backup put-backup-vault-access-policy` to weaken the policy. Both should be denied by the compliance-mode lock. Evidence: CLI denial output, CloudTrail event IDs, finding spreadsheet for any vault that allows the action."
AWS-081	AWS	Single-account backup is a single attack target for ransomware-style adversaries.	TC1. The tester reads each backup plan's `CopyActions` via `aws backup get-backup-plan --backup-plan-id <id>` to identify the destination backup vault and target account. The destination vault's access policy is pulled via `aws backup get-backup-vault-access-policy --backup-vault-name <v>`. Plans copying only to the same account or to vaults with permissive access policies are flagged. Evidence: copy-action inventory CSV, destination account / policy matrix.
AWS-082	AWS	DR readiness is what determines recovery time under a successful attack.	TC1. The tester enumerates the DR region resource set via Steampipe / `aws resourcegroupstaggingapi get-resources --region <dr-region>` and confirms KMS multi-region key replication via `aws kms describe-key --key-id <id> --query 'KeyMetadata.MultiRegion'`. Critical resources missing in DR or KMS keys not replicated are flagged. Evidence: DR-vs-prod resource diff CSV, KMS replication-state matrix, finding spreadsheet.
DETEC-001	DETECTION	Defines where every detection validation case is observed and timed.	TC1. Enumerate SIEM platform, version and operating model via cloud APIs and document baseline.
DETEC-002	DETECTION	Volume affects detection latency, cost-of-rule decisions and false-positive tolerance.	TC1. Enumerate SIEM ingestion volume and growth trend via cloud APIs and document baseline.
DETEC-003	DETECTION	Each gap becomes a blind spot the adversary will eventually find.	"TC1. Enumerate source-by-source ingestion mode and latency via cloud APIs and document baseline.
TC2. Send canary event to each source and measure end-to-end SIEM latency."
