GOVER-001	GOVERNANCE	Without an explicit, signed authorization no offensive activity may begin against any cloud asset.	TC1. Acting as the engagement lead, the tester cross-walks every AWS account ID returned by `aws organizations list-accounts`, every Azure subscription ID returned by `az account list`, and every M365 tenant ID returned by Microsoft Graph `/v1.0/organization` against the executed authorization letter on file. Any cloud identifier present in the technical inventory but absent from the signed letter is flagged as out-of-scope until written authorization is obtained. The tester captures a side-by-side spreadsheet with cloud-identifier, owner, signature date and authorization-letter PDF hash. Evidence: signed letter PDFs (SHA-256), inventory CSV, and the gap list shared with the bank's CISO and legal counsel.
GOVER-002	GOVERNANCE	GCC banking regulators expect prior notification of offensive testing on systems-of-record.	TC1. The tester reviews the bank's Compliance / Regulatory Affairs ticket for the CBB (or equivalent GCC regulator) pre-test notification, confirming the template covers offensive activity scope, test windows, tester company name, and points-of-contact. The tester captures the sent date, the regulator acknowledgement reference, and any conditions the regulator attached (e.g., no production data exfiltration). Where notification is overdue, the tester defers all live production-cloud activity and works with Compliance to file the notification before resuming. Evidence: regulator submission PDF, acknowledgement reference / email, and Compliance officer sign-off recorded in the engagement RACI.
GOVER-003	GOVERNANCE	Some test activity requires the cloud provider to be notified or pre-approved before execution.	"TC1. The tester completes the Microsoft Cloud Penetration Testing Rules of Engagement at aka.ms/mspentest declaring tenant ID, in-scope subscriptions, test windows, and a list of techniques that include consent-phishing simulation, attempted Defender for Cloud evasion, and any traffic that may pattern-match DDoS. The tester archives the auto-generated submission ID and Microsoft acknowledgement email. Where Microsoft requests clarification, the tester pauses any pre-approved-only activity until response is received. Evidence: ROE form PDF, submission reference, and Microsoft response thread.
TC2. The tester opens an AWS Support case under the Customer Support Penetration Testing Acknowledgement category for each in-scope account, listing simulated-event types such as port-and-protocol scans above the 1 Gbps soft threshold, simulated DNS-based exfiltration to tester-owned domains, and any iperf-style throughput tests. The tester records the AWS case ID, the assigned support engineer and the response disposition. Evidence: AWS case PDF, case ID and disposition email."
GOVER-004	GOVERNANCE	Testing into shared tenants requires that party's prior written authorization.	TC1. The tester enumerates shared-tenant boundaries by walking AWS Organizations RAM shares with `aws ram list-resources`, Azure cross-tenant access policies via Graph `/v1.0/policies/crossTenantAccessPolicy/partners`, M365 B2B configurations and any documented payment-processor / SWIFT / fintech connections in the bank's third-party register. Each boundary is mapped to a partner legal entity and authorization status. The tester pauses any technique that would cross a boundary without a partner authorization letter on file and routes the gap to the bank's vendor security manager. Evidence: boundary inventory CSV, RAM share JSON, cross-tenant policy JSON, and pause-decision log.
GOVER-005	GOVERNANCE	Evidence often contains regulated data and must be handled, stored and destroyed under the right legal cover.	TC1. The tester walks the bank's data-classification matrix (Public / Internal / Confidential / Highly Confidential / Regulated-PCI / Regulated-PII / Shariah-Board) against the executed Master NDA and DPA clauses, flagging any class that may surface in screenshots, command transcripts, KQL output or CloudTrail extracts. Where a class lacks explicit cover, the tester drafts an addendum and routes it to bank legal counsel before any test that would touch that class. The tester also confirms cross-border transfer wording covers offshore evidence handling in the tester's SOC. Evidence: data-class-to-clause matrix, drafted addendum (if any), counsel sign-off email.
GOVER-006	GOVERNANCE	Undeclared testing can void coverage during an unrelated future incident.	TC1. The tester reviews the bank's active cyber-insurance policy schedule with the bank's broker, identifies the offensive-testing clause and any notification SLA, and obtains a written broker confirmation that the engagement dates, scope and tester identity are covered. Where the policy requires advance notification, the tester drafts the notification and tracks the broker's acknowledgement. Evidence: redacted policy clause extract, broker confirmation email, notification PDF, and acknowledgement timestamp.
GOVER-007	GOVERNANCE	All testing must occur inside windows that minimise impact to live banking operations.	TC1. The tester raises a change-management ticket in the bank's CAB tooling (ServiceNow / Remedy) for the approved test window covering date range, daily hours, weekday vs weekend handling, prayer-time pauses and Friday-handling rules. The CAB ticket is circulated to SOC, Cloud Operations, Network Operations and Application Operations leads with explicit acknowledgement requested. The tester embeds the window into the runbook's automated stop rules. Evidence: CAB ticket reference, distribution acknowledgement, and the tester runbook snippet enforcing the window.
GOVER-008	GOVERNANCE	Testing during business-critical windows risks misattributing real incidents to the engagement.	TC1. The tester collects black-out periods from Treasury (settlement windows), Finance (month / quarter / year-end close), Regulatory Reporting (CBB cut-offs), HR (Eid / Hajj) and Operations (peak ATM days) and codes them into the engagement automation as hard-stop windows enforced by the tester orchestration layer. Any technique scheduled to fire inside a black-out is automatically held and re-queued. The tester confirms the list with the bank's change-management chair before live testing. Evidence: black-out calendar CSV, runbook snippet enforcing the hold, and chair sign-off email.
GOVER-009	GOVERNANCE	An immediate, well-rehearsed stop process is mandatory for live cloud adversarial testing.	TC1. Before any live cloud activity, the tester runs a kill-switch dry run by triggering the agreed channel (24x7 phone bridge, SMS rota, and Teams channel) and measuring time-to-acknowledge plus time-to-full-stop across the tester team. The drill is repeated until the stop SLA is met for both phone and Teams paths. The tester documents named invokers on the bank side (SOC tier-3, CISO, Cloud Operations on-call) and the corresponding tester escalation tree. Evidence: drill timing log, channel test screenshots, and signed kill-switch SOP.
GOVER-010	GOVERNANCE	Predictable communication keeps executives, SOC and engineering aligned during the engagement.	TC1. The tester publishes a communications plan covering daily 09:00 stand-up on a named Teams channel, a weekly Friday status email to the CISO and engagement sponsor, and an end-of-test debrief workshop. Primary and backup contacts are nominated on both sides with phone, mobile and Teams handles. Language defaults to English; Arabic exec summary added on request. Evidence: communications plan PDF, contact RACI, and acknowledgement from each named contact.
GOVER-011	GOVERNANCE	Blind testing produces a more realistic measure of detection but raises operational risk.	TC1. The tester walks the CISO and SOC manager through the trade-off between white-team (SOC informed up-front, faster triage, less realism) and true purple / blind (SOC discovers via normal detection, realistic TTD measurement, higher operational risk) and captures a signed decision memo. The chosen posture is reflected in the runbook automation, the watermark distribution and the daily comms cadence. Evidence: signed decision memo, runbook posture flag, and watermark distribution list.
GOVER-012	GOVERNANCE	Attribution markers prevent confusion between tester traffic and real adversary activity post-engagement.	TC1. The tester generates a unique watermark string (e.g., `NST-PENTEST-2026-Q2-<engagement-id>`) and embeds it into every tester user-agent, IAM session-name (`aws sts assume-role --role-session-name <watermark>`), Azure CLI user-agent override, Microsoft Graph client-name and PowerShell module banner. The SOC is given a KQL hunting query `union * | where * has 'NST-PENTEST-2026'` and an Athena query over CloudTrail to retrieve every tester event. The tester confirms hits land in Sentinel and CloudTrail / CloudTrail Lake before live testing. Evidence: watermark string spec, Sentinel watchlist entry ID, sample matched events from each platform.
GOVER-013	GOVERNANCE	Tester accounts shape the assumed-breach personas used during emulation.	"TC1. The tester reviews each provisioning ticket in the bank's IGA tool (SailPoint / Saviynt / ServiceNow IAM) for federated AWS Identity Center accounts, Entra ID accounts and M365 accounts assigned to NetSentries staff. The approval chain (line manager, application owner, CISO) is confirmed and the final role / permission-set assignments are exported via `aws sso-admin list-account-assignments`, Microsoft Graph `/v1.0/roleManagement/directory/roleAssignments` and `Get-MgUser`. The tester maps every account to its persona (Section 3 of the engagement plan). Evidence: provisioning ticket IDs, exported assignment JSON, persona-to-account map.
TC2. The tester drives each provisioned account through its planned starting-state actions (e.g., Entra user can read `/me`, AWS federated role can call `sts get-caller-identity` and `iam:ListUsers` but not `iam:CreateUser`) using AWS CLI `--dry-run` where supported and `aws iam simulate-principal-policy` for non-dry-run paths. Any action that should be allowed but is denied, or denied but allowed, is logged for re-provisioning. Evidence: simulate-principal-policy outputs, command transcripts, and the resulting starting-state delta report."
GOVER-014	GOVERNANCE	Persona-aligned accounts make emulation realistic for the blast-radius of an actual employee compromise.	"TC1. The tester collaborates with IAM and HR to map each engagement scenario (HR analyst compromise, junior DevOps token theft, treasury operator phishing, third-party contractor abuse) to a real production permission shape. For each persona the tester captures the AD group memberships, Entra role assignments, AWS Identity Center permission set and any SaaS app entitlements via tools like AzureHound, BloodHound for Azure and ScoutSuite. Evidence: persona-to-role map spreadsheet, group-membership exports, and the matching IGA approval ticket per persona.
TC2. Once persona accounts are live, the tester drives ATT&CK lateral-movement techniques (T1021.007 Cloud Services, T1550.001 Application Access Token, T1078.004 Cloud Accounts) only from inside that persona's effective permission set, using Pacu's `iam__privesc_scan`, BloodHound queries and Azure ROADtools traversal. Any movement that the persona's permissions should deny is treated as a finding; any movement that succeeds is logged with the source persona and target. Evidence: BloodHound paths, Pacu module outputs, CloudTrail / Entra audit logs of each attempted move."
GOVER-015	GOVERNANCE	Endpoint posture and egress controls materially affect what tradecraft is realistic.	TC1. The tester validates that every operating endpoint (bank-issued PAW, bank VDI via Citrix / AVD, or NetSentries hardened laptop on ZTNA) matches the agreed origin model documented in the engagement plan. Posture checks include disk-encryption state, EDR enrolment (`Get-MpComputerStatus` on Windows, `mdatp health` on macOS), tester-only admin accounts, and a clean network-egress IP captured via `curl https://ifconfig.me`. Where ZTNA / VPN is used the tester confirms the assigned source IP matches the allow-list and that split-tunnelling is configured per policy. Evidence: posture screenshots, EDR enrolment IDs, source-IP capture.
GOVER-016	GOVERNANCE	Existing geo / risk policies may block testers before any technique can be evaluated.	TC1. The tester captures the agreed source IP / CIDR ranges (NetSentries SOC egress, ZTNA exit, fallback office IPs) and walks them through the bank's Conditional Access named-location set (Entra `/v1.0/identity/conditionalAccess/namedLocations`), AWS WAF IP-set rules, Azure Front Door allow-lists and any third-party WAF (Cloudflare / Akamai) IP rules. Each change is raised as a tracked CR with a documented rollback (`az network firewall-policy ... delete` / `aws wafv2 update-ip-set` revert). At end-of-engagement the tester runs the rollback CR and confirms the IPs are no longer present. Evidence: CR ticket IDs, before / after IP-set JSON, rollback CR completion.
GOVER-017	GOVERNANCE	Identical MFA posture ensures bypass tradecraft is tested against the real control set.	"TC1. The tester enumerates every Conditional Access policy via Microsoft Graph `/v1.0/identity/conditionalAccess/policies` and the tester user / group membership against the policy includes / excludes blocks. AzureHound `azurehound list policies` and Maester (`Invoke-Maester`) are run to cross-check exemptions. Any tester identity excluded from MFA, device-compliance or sign-in-risk policies is flagged so it can be re-included before live testing. Evidence: full CA policy export JSON, Maester report, gap list.
TC2. Emulating T1621 MFA Request Generation, the tester uses MFASweep against an opt-in tester account followed by 5-10 paced authentication attempts via Microsoft Authenticator push to drive MFA-fatigue, with the tester device the only registered authenticator. The tester captures whether number-match is enforced (preventing accidental approval), whether Defender XDR raises an MFA-fatigue alert, and whether Sentinel KQL `SigninLogs | where ResultType == 50074` fires. Push sequence stops at 10 prompts or first SOC alert. Evidence: MFA prompt screenshots, Defender alert ID, Sentinel incident ID, sign-in log entries."
GOVER-018	GOVERNANCE	Banking data sovereignty rules restrict where regulated evidence may be processed and stored.	TC1. The tester confirms with the bank's Data Protection Officer the in-scope evidence repository — typically a NetSentries SharePoint / OneDrive site with sensitivity labelling, Customer-Lockbox style approvals and AES-256 at rest — plus a backup encrypted-VeraCrypt container on the tester PAW. The access list is enumerated via `Get-MgGroupMember` for the SharePoint group and reviewed against named tester staff only. Evidence: repository site URL, sensitivity label configuration, access list export, DPO sign-off email.
GOVER-019	GOVERNANCE	Regional residency rules require evidence with customer data to remain in jurisdiction.	TC1. The tester applies the bank's data classification at evidence capture time — every screenshot, command transcript and exported JSON is tagged via the tester's Purview-equivalent labelling profile and a pre-commit hook in the evidence-collection script (`evidence-tag.py`) blocks any artefact missing a classification. Where regulated PII / cardholder data appears, the tester redacts via `pdfredact-tools` and stores the original in an in-country encrypted container. Evidence: tagged artefact tree, redaction manifest, in-country storage location reference.
